# A temporal operator inside a Dwyer pattern.
#
# Pattern operands used to have to be state formulas: `O(...)` in a body was
# rejected outright, while `Xs`/`Ys` were let through, so a one-step lookback
# compiled and an unbounded one did not. Both are allowed now, and the lowering
# decides where the operator is evaluated: under `globally` a pattern spans the
# whole trace and the operator is buffered as usual, while inside a scope the
# body is re-evaluated over each segment and the operator reads only that
# segment.
#
# The trace is 26 states, state k having only the k-th letter true, so `a`
# holds exactly at the first state and `z` exactly at the last. Every
# requirement below must hold.

data    a:  boolean;
data    b:  boolean;
data    c:  boolean;
data    d:  boolean;
data    e:  boolean;
data    f:  boolean;
data    g:  boolean;
data    h:  boolean;
data    i:  boolean;
data    j:  boolean;
data    k:  boolean;
data    l:  boolean;
data    m:  boolean;
data    n:  boolean;
data    o:  boolean;
data    p:  boolean;
data    q:  boolean;
data    r:  boolean;
data    s:  boolean;
data    t:  boolean;
data    u:  boolean;
data    v:  boolean;
data    w:  boolean;
data    x:  boolean;
data    y:  boolean;
data    z:  boolean;

#   Whole trace: `a` is the first state, so it is in the past of every state.
@glob_once
globally, it is always the case that O(a) holds;

#   `b` is false at the first state, so "b has always held" is false everywhere.
@glob_hist
globally, it is never the case that H(b) holds;

#   Every state has `z` either ahead of it or behind it.
@glob_fut
globally, it is always the case that F(z) || O(z) holds;

#   A temporal operand in a response, not just in a universality body.
@glob_response
globally, if b, then in response O(a) eventually holds;

#   An accumulator is a temporal operand too: from any state, at least one
#   state remains to be counted.
@glob_count
globally, it is always the case that Cnt(true) >= 1 holds;

#   The step operators stay as they were -- `z` is the last state, so no state
#   has it as its predecessor.
@glob_step
globally, it is never the case that Ys(z) holds;

#   Scoped: the operator sees the segment, not the trace. `a` is at the first
#   state, which is outside every segment below, so a whole-trace `O(a)` would
#   be true at each of them and the segment-relative one is false.
@after_once
after c, it is never the case that O(a) holds;

@after_hist
after c, it is always the case that H(!a) holds;

#   A future operator under a scope whose segment runs to the end of the trace.
@after_fut
after c, z eventually holds;

#   `before` opens at the start of the trace, so `a` *is* in this segment.
@before_once
before c, it is always the case that O(a) holds;

#   A `between` segment opens at `c`, which is therefore in its own past.
@between_once
between c and e, it is always the case that O(c) holds;

@between_none
between c and e, it is never the case that O(a) holds;

#   An accumulator inside a segment counts only the segment's states.
@between_count
between c and e, it is always the case that Cnt(a) == 0 holds;

@while_once
while c, it is always the case that O(c) holds;

@after_until_none
after c until e, it is never the case that O(a) holds;

#   Nested scopes: the innermost segment is what the operator reads.
@nested
after c, between d and f, it is never the case that O(a) holds;

#   A `before` nested inside another scope: it pushes the segment's `last`, and
#   used to pop the enclosing one along with it.
@nested_before
after c, before f, it is never the case that O(a) holds;
